      "message": "Hi Sarah,\n\nApologies for the delay \u2014 the corrected invoice #2214 for $4,200 (July engagement) will be issued and uploaded to the portal by tomorrow, EOD. That should give you plenty of time ahead of Friday's payment run close.\n\nI'll send a confirmation once it's posted so you can verify the updated amount right away.\n\nBest,\nImpact Team"
    },
    {
      "to": "Miguel Alvarez <miguel.alvarez@stateu.edu>",
      "subject": "Re: Guest lecture on agentic programming?",
      "message": "Hi Miguel,\n\nThank you for reaching out \u2014 we'd be delighted to give a guest lecture for your AI systems seminar. It's great to hear that some of your students are already experimenting with Jaseci; that familiarity will let us go deeper into the agentic programming concepts rather than starting from scratch.\n\nSeptember 22 works well for us. We're happy to deliver the lecture remotely during the 2\u20134pm Eastern window. One of our team members will lead the 60-minute session, covering core agentic programming ideas through the lens of Jaseci \u2014 including the Jac programming language, graph-based program structure (nodes, edges, and walkers), and how it all fits into scale-native cloud deployment. We'll tailor the level to your graduate students and leave time for Q&A.\n\nI'll follow up shortly to confirm the specific speaker and share any materials you'd like to distribute to students ahead of time. If a video platform link or any other logistics would be helpful from our end, just let us know.\n\nLooking forward to it!\n\nBest regards,\nThe Jaseci Team\nimpact@jaseci.org"
    },
    {
      "to": "Priya Nair <priya.nair@devfleet.io>",
      "subject": "Re: Quick question | byLLM structured output",
      "message": "Hi Priya,\n\nYes \u2014 byLLM supports typed/structured outputs natively, so you don't need to parse the model's text output yourself.\n\nUsing the `by llm()` syntax in Jac, you can declare a typed return value that matches your desired schema. The runtime enforces that the model output conforms to the declared type, so your walker can return structured fields directly. For your use case, you'd define a type with severity, component, and message fields, and the model output will be parsed into those fields automatically.\n\nExample shape:\n\n```\ntype log_entry {\n    has severity: str;\n    has component: str;\n    has message: str;\n}\n```\n\nYour walker can then return a `log_entry` via `by llm()`, and each log line like `ERROR | walker_spawn | context missing` will be structured into the three fields without manual text parsing.\n\nLet me know if you'd like a fuller code sample or help wiring this into your triage pipeline.\n\nBest,\nJaseci Team"
    }
  ],
